8 Elementary Operations and Row Reduction

The elementary row operations on \(A\in M_{n\times m}(F)\) are:

\(R_i=r_i+\lambda r_j\) for \(\lambda\in F\) and \(j\neq i\) (add a multiple of another row);
\(R_i=\lambda r_i\) for \(0\neq\lambda\in F\) (scale a row);
\(R_i\leftrightarrow R_j\) (swap rows),

where \(r_i\) denotes the row before the operation and \(R_i\) the row after. Column operations are defined similarly. Matrices related by a sequence of elementary operations are row-equivalent.


A matrix is in reduced row echelon form if

every zero row is below every nonzero row;
the leading (first nonzero) entry of each nonzero row is \(1\), and these move strictly to the right as we go down the rows;
each leading \(1\) is the only nonzero entry in its column.


Every matrix can be put in reduced row echelon form by elementary row operations (Gaussian elimination). Row operations correspond to multiplying on the left by invertible matrices, so they do not change the solution set of \(A\vec{x}=\vec{b}\) (applied to the augmented matrix \([A\mid\vec{b}]\)). A homogeneous system with more unknowns than equations therefore always has a nonzero solution, since a free column remains.

