Given linear vector space \(\mathcal{V}\) an inner product \(\left&lt; \cdot, \cdot \right&gt;:\mathcal{V}\times\mathcal{V}
\to \mathbb{R}\) is a mapping satisfying the following properties for all \(v_{1}, v_{2}, v_{3} \in \mathcal{V}\):

Symmetry: \(\left&lt; v_{1}, v_{2} \right&gt; = \left&lt; v_{2}, v_{1} \right&gt;\);
Non-negativity: \(\left&lt; v_{1}, v_{1} \right&gt; \geq 0\);
Non-Degeneracy: \(\left&lt; v_{1}, v_{1} \right&gt; =0 \iff v_{1}=0\); and
Linearity: \(\left&lt;  \alpha v_{1}, v_{2} + v_{3} \right&gt; =
\alpha \left&lt;  v_{1}, v_{2} \right&gt; + \alpha \left&lt; v_{1}, v_{3} \right&gt; =
\alpha \left&lt;  v_{1}, v_{2} + v_{3} \right&gt;.\)


Note: Every inner product creates a norm satisfying: \(\lVert v \rVert = \sqrt{ \left&lt; v, v \right&gt; }.\)

